5417] [17592186159730 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159730 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "128c143c-1206-5a0d-92ce-dad3f6df6aa1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159731 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{8}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159731 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159731 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2cac8f41-fff7-5b34-ae38-570dd1faab05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159732 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{16}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159732 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159732 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3396fd2c-6299-532f-81b0-89d0d78d2a17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159733 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159733 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159733 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "72d5813b-32a6-53c1-b39a-47c432820dd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161690 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10303" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161690 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F\\left(2,1\\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161690 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4b51a4b-0571-55e9-a3ea-881ffce4839b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161690 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,1) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X \\leq 2, Y \\leq 1.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,1) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 1}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,-2)+f(1,-1)+f(1,0) \\\\  & =0.1+0.3+0.05 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161691 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161691 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) be ![](images/45/45ba004dac2f7f8efb58a5071b600dded19307f6fbc2bc376cc780ec2054b4fa.svg) where\n\n![](images/47/47adc5d8b26f9b62b8f051b7b68b7100060841ecb29cde0e38e7b417d0373855.svg)\n\nand the supports of ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg), respectively, are\n\n![](images/c2/c2eae5ac498b77fffad4a4903f54ec0a15c51a7477f84215483752bdeeef0bab.svg)\n\nFind ![](images/4c/4c28154b55d157211d489b908d786504dd07515970926a784570fad2911cc3dd.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161691 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "115c9ea4-0415-5249-8a4f-a7113e022681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161691 #edb/ref 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF ![](images/7f/7f3705d5dab288c1c6774f437aa18dea961252d741fb302b399cb2ad18112a3a.svg) we can think of each point where ![](images/d1/d17e98e4e46a13b4d2d8f97bbcf008c339ed281a929aa3eec7ecdd7b63a9ac92.svg) as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF ![](images/24/2