 Two Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162165 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186163101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162165 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162165 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162165 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5a4d4eca-048a-559f-8986-fcbff586afd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162411 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162411 #edb/ref 1069 "q-145559" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162411 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162411 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162411 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "579f1fda-9534-54f2-9a64-f554fdec8e40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162411 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162411 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 4.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 4}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,2)+f(1,4)+f(2,2)+f(2,4) \\\\  & =0.1+0.25+0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163101 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163101 #edb/ref 1061 38838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163101 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "494e3329-5465-5724-b614-4ecacfaaed97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163101 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163102 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163102 #edb/ref 1061 38839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163102 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f666bcd7-dca8-55b3-aedf-a8ef91edbe96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163102 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163103 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163103 #edb/ref 1061 38840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163103 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59dbcbcc-3e03-5e5e-946c-753994f1060e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163103 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163108 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163108 #edb/ref 1061 38938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163108 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ccba458-0f60-53d1-9e72-3de4cf2a6b1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163108 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163109 #edb/ref 1061 38939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163109 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2851f1ef-e083-54cd-a8c0-7dd89a1c3344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163109 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163152 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163152 #edb/ref 1061 42192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163152 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d0f7f20-6219-5e6b-9d38-ad689f056506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163152 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163341 #edb/ref 1064 "The **joint cumulative distribution function** (or joint CDF) of two discrete random variables $X,$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P( X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)  = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nThe sets $S_X$ and $S_Y,$ on which the variables $X$ and $Y$ are defined, are the **supports** of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWe can see from this definition that the joint CDF is analogous to the cumulative distribution function of a single random variable.\n\nConsider the joint PMF for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nHere, the supports of $X$ and $Y$ are $S_X = \\{ 0, 1 \\}$ and $S_Y = \\{ 0, 1, 2 \\},$ respectively. \n\nAs an example, let's compute $F(1,1).$ By definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X \\leq 1, Y \\leq 1.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =f(0,0)+f(0,1)+f(1,0)+f(1,1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.3+0.2 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163341 #edb/ref 1065 6430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163341 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "68282cd4-eb49-54e1-a97a-8d49988c8922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163341 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163360 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the joint PMF for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWe can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\neq 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n![](images/c6/c6d8e4012a0a16f865bf229549512ff772e103d3fcd7667738cf7250d7267e84.png)\n\n \n\nNow, the joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nGeometrically, then, the joint CDF gives the total \"probability mass\" that lies inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nFor example, consider the point $(2,1.5)$ and the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at this point, shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46837da3e8f8f44831da4e6b464e9f8562f322b17f584df3fc87912d1c09d4c4.png)\n\nThe value of $F(2,1.5)$ equals the total \"probability mass\" contained within this rectangle. That is,\n\n$$\nF(2,1.5) = f(1,0) + f(2,0) + f(1,1) + f(2,1).\n$$\n\nIt's worth highlighting a few important cases:\n\n- If our rectangular region doesn't include any points where the probability is nonzero, then we must have $F(x,y) = 0.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb5faa2b42c9ece2fe80cff4dd27a8b720cb363f5319c3cb5f5dd065f818c403.png)\n\n- If our rectangular region includes *all* points where the probability is nonzero, then we must have $F(x,y) = 1.$\n\n![](images/81/811b15e3cea46e0a4a546e68217a2994a7b2a7b80d6bc444bc6fbaa2ebaed355.png)\n\nFinally, we note the following properties of the joint CDF:\n\n- $0 \\leq F(x,y) \\leq 1$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by $F_X(x) = F(x, \\infty).$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $Y$ is given by $F_Y(y) = F( \\infty, y).$\n\n- $F(\\infty, \\infty) = 1$\n\n- $F(-\\infty,y) = F(x, -\\infty) = 0$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then $F(x,y) = F_X(x) \\cdot F_Y(y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163360 #edb/ref 1065 6977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163360 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d6a285ac-6fdf-506c-9228-20857fedc5b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163360 #edb/ref 1067 "Geometric Interpretation of the Joint CDF" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480745 #edb/ref 1080 "0.23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480745 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480745 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04ed47b4-e775-566f-bc2c-56a2a7bded91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480748 #edb/ref 1080 "0.34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480748 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480748 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05a1a33d-ed6a-57ac-b7c7-d80e7811473a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480779 #edb/ref 1080 "0.6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480779 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480779 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1657cbc2-f103-5ad9-8c0b-3b96b07759b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480812 #edb/ref 1080 "0.4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480812 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480812 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2734458c-e754-5af7-aae6-6e7c43061d34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480822 #edb/ref 1080 "0.35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480822 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480822 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2bbc5089-2da3-5298-974d-ece99d9afcd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480860 #edb/ref 1080 "0.6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480860 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480860 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e0814a4-0dea-5ca4-b7fc-9b692b925da5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480865 #edb/ref 1080 "0.3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480865 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480865 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4231ab6f-77a8-55e4-aea1-903e92ce3f2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480873 #edb/ref 1080 "0.6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480873 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480873 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4703c062-7fb3-5337-a7b8-1155f59f3774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480900 #edb/ref 1080 "0.05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480900 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480900 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "58bac764-44f8-55c1-ba07-ab0f55dee029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480903 #edb/ref 1080 "0.35" #edb/ref 17592186